```{r setup, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", error = FALSE,
                      render = knitr::normal_print)
old_options = options(gptr.quiet = TRUE, cli.unicode = FALSE, cli.num_colors = 1, width = 80)
```

gptr runs a language model agent inside your R session. The agent works on the objects that are
already in memory: it inspects them, runs R code on them and leaves its results in your
workspace, so a large object is loaded once and a mistake costs one re-evaluation instead of a
fresh run of the whole script. The same function, `peter()`, is an interactive chat at the
console and a programmable call in scripts, loops and `if` statements.

Peter's name commemorates Peter Naur and Peter Cathcart Wason. The
[Why Peter? guide](why-peter.html) connects their work on programs and reasoning to gptr.

This vignette runs without a network connection or a key. Every call uses
`gptr_fake_provider()`, a scripted model that ships with the package, so the output is
reproducible. Start with this example before connecting an API, subscription command-line tool
or local Ollama model through the [language-model guide](language-models.html).

## A first call

A fake provider plays a script in which each element is one model reply. A reply can call a
tool: the `r` tool runs R code in the environment you give to `peter()`.

```{r}
library(gptr)

fake = gptr_fake_provider(list(
  list(tool = "r", input = list(code = paste(
    "fit = lm(mpg ~ wt, data = cars)", "gptr_return(coef(fit))", sep = "\n"
  ))),
  "Each additional 1000 lb of weight lowers fuel economy by about 5.3 miles per gallon."
))

work = new.env()
work$cars = mtcars
s = peter("Fit mpg on wt in cars and return the coefficients.", cars = work$cars,
          model = fake, envir = work, mode = auto)
s$text
s$value
```

Prompts are always quoted strings. Identifiers such as the mode can be bare names, the way
`library(gptr)` needs no quotes: `mode = auto` and `mode = "auto"` are the same. The `auto` mode
lets ordinary agent actions run without asking, while retaining the critical and secret guards.
This vignette needs it because nobody is there to answer. At the console the default mode,
`manual`, asks before changes unless a permission rule already allows them. The fake provider
supplies the tool code explicitly; a real model chooses its own code from the prompt.

`s$text` is the final answer, and `s$value` is the value the tool selected with `gptr_return()`.
The object the agent created is in `work`, like any object you created yourself:

```{r}
ls(work)
coef(work$fit)
```

`peter()` returned a session object. It holds the conversation, the model, the status and the
token usage:

```{r}
s$status
s$turns
gptr_usage(s, by = "model")
```

## The pipe steers one session

Piping a session into `peter()` adds a turn to the same session: the same history, the same
model and the same workspace. Nothing is copied and no new conversation starts.

```{r}
fake2 = gptr_fake_provider(list(
  list(tool = "r", input = list(code = "cyl_mpg = tapply(mtcars$mpg, mtcars$cyl, mean)")),
  "The mean fuel economy per number of cylinders is stored in `cyl_mpg`.",
  "Four-cylinder cars are the most economical, at about 26.7 miles per gallon.",
  "Four."
))

s2 = peter("Compute the mean mpg for each number of cylinders.", model = fake2, envir = work,
          mode = auto)
s3 = s2 |> peter("Which group is the most economical?")
identical(s2, s3)
s2$turns
s2$text
```

A branch is always explicit. `gptr_fork()` copies the conversation into a new session whose
workspace sees the original objects without copying them and keeps its own changes:

```{r}
branch = gptr_fork(s2)
branch |> peter("Answer in one word.")
c(original = s2$turns, branch = branch$turns)
```

## Stopping instead of guessing

In `manual` mode a change needs approval unless an allow rule already permits it. When nobody
can answer (an `Rscript` job, a knitted report), the run stops with a classed condition that says
how to allow the action:

```{r}
careful = gptr_fake_provider(list(
  list(tool = "r", input = list(code = "mtcars$kpl = mtcars$mpg * 0.425")),
  "Added the column kpl."
))
res = tryCatch(
  peter("Add fuel economy in km per liter to mtcars.", model = careful, envir = new.env(),
       mode = manual),
  gptr_error_permission = function(e) e
)
class(res)[1]
res$session$status
```

`mode = plan` inspects and proposes while preserving the user workspace; level-1 R exploration
can create intermediate objects in its scratch environment. `mode = edits` allows file edits
inside the project, and `gptr_permissions()` adds rules such as `"r(level<=1)"` that allow
low-risk code without a question.

## At the console

Called without a prompt in an interactive console, `peter()` opens a chat. Piping a session
into it continues that session, and `/exit` returns the session. Inside chat, `/help` lists
commands, `@name` attaches an object and `!code` runs your own R immediately. The
[console guide](interactive-console.html) explains input syntax, permissions and interrupts.

```{r, eval = FALSE}
peter()
s |> peter()
```

## Connecting a real model

Choose a route explicitly. API providers use their named environment variables, which you can
load from a private file with `gptr_env()`. Subscription CLI routes use the executable's own
sign-in; Ollama uses an already-running local server and an installed model. The
[language-model guide](language-models.html) gives setup examples for each route.

```{r, eval = FALSE}
gptr_env("~/keys/.env")
gptr_providers()
gptr_models(provider = "openai")
gptr_config(model = "openai/gpt-6-sol", .scope = "user")

res = peter("Describe the mpg and wt columns.", mtcars)
res$text
```

The first time a provider is used interactively, gptr shows what is sent to it and records your
acknowledgment; `?gptr_egress` lists what a request contains. For persistent storage, call
`gptr_init("/path/to/project")` to create a `.gptr/` workspace and review its separate trust
decision. Without a project workspace, session storage is temporary. Defaults and storage
policy are covered in the [configuration guide](configuration.html).

